Anvaya: madhyaspaṣṭayuterdalonitacalaṃ kujāt ceṣṭākhyakendraṃ syāt. tat ceṣṭākendraṃ ṣaḍadhikaṃ cet tadā bhagaṇāccyutaṃ ṣaḍhṛt ceṣṭābalaṃ syāt. ekottaraṃ rūpaṃ adrivihṛtaṃ kramāt mandārajñasurejyaśukraśaśabhṛttīkṣṇadyutīnāṃ naisargikaṃ balaṃ syāt.

Translation: Half the sum of the mean and true longitude subtracted from the sighrocca is the Cheshta kendra for grahas starting from Kuja. If that Cheshta kendra is greater than six, then subtracted from a bhagana and divided by six, it becomes the Cheshtabala. One, increasing by one successively, divided by seven, becomes the Naisargikabala for Manda, Ara, Jna, Surejya, Shukra, Shashabhrit, and Tikshnadyuti in order.

Commentary: madhyaspaṣṭayuterdalonitacalaṃ = half the sum of the mean and true graha subtracted from the cala = sighrocca, kujāt = starting from Kuja, is the ceṣṭākhyakendra. tat ceṣṭākendraṃ ṣaḍadhikam = if greater than six rashis, tadā bhagaṇād = then from twelve rashis cyutaṃ = subtracted (purified), ṣaḍhṛt becomes the ceṣṭābala. ekottaraṃ rūpaṃ adrivihṛtaṃ = divided by seven, kramāt mandārajñasurejyaśukraśaśabhṛttīkṣṇadyutīnāṃ naisargikaṃ = natural balaṃ syāt. For example, rūpaṃ = one divided by seven is the bala of Manda (Shani), similarly two divided by seven is the bala of Bhauma (Mangala), three divided by seven is the bala of Budha, and thus it should be understood for all.

